ULondon), António Guedes de Oliveira (CMUP, FCUP), Domingos Cardoso (CIDMA, UA), António Breda D'azevedo (CIDMA, UA), Catarina Cruz (CIDMA, UA), Charles Dominic (UA), Domenico Antonino Catalano (CIDMA, UA), Dominika Kubijk (UA), Enide Andrade Martins (CIDMA, UA), Henrique Cruz (UBI), Ilda Rodrigues (UBI), Inês Soares (INESCC, UC), Jorge Orestes Cerdeira (Centro de Estudos Florestais, FCT-UNL), José Luís Fachada (IST), Łukasz Witkowski (UA), Leonor Moreira (FCUP), Maria Elisa Fernandes (CIDMA, UA), Mário Rosa (UC), Olga Azenhas (CMUC, FCTUC), Paula Carvalho (CIDMA, UA), Paula Rama (CIDMA, UA), Paulo Almeida (CIDMA, UA), Raul Cordovil (IST), Ricardo Mamede (CMUC, FCTUC), Pedro J.\nL52: Freitas (CELC, FCUL), Rogério Serôdio (UBI), Rosário Pinto (CMUP,FCUP), Rui Duarte (CIDMA, UA), Rute Lemos (CIDMA, UA), Samuel Lopes (CMUP, FCUP), Sofia Pinheiro (CIDMA, UA).\nL53: Organisers: Rui Duarte (CIDMA,UA), António Guedes de Oliveira (CMUP,FC-UP), and Olga Azenhas (CMUC,UC)\nL54: \nL55: Sponsors: 【8† Departamento de Matemática †www.ua.pt】 da 【9†Universidade de Aveiro†www.ua.pt】, 【10† CIM†www.cim.pt】, 【11† CMUP†cmup.fc.up.pt】, and 【12† CIDMA†cidma.mat.ua.pt】.\nL56: \nL57: To participate send an email to 4thcombday@ua.pt\nL58: \nL59:    Design by 【13†Reality Software†www.realitysoftware.ca】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.singacom.uva.es/Uploads/2012/504875720c383.pdf)\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 8; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.singacom.uva.es/Uploads/2012/504875720c383.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 703\nL0@P0: This article appeared in a journal published by Elsevier. The attached\nL1@P0: copy is furnished to the author for internal non-commercial research\nL2@P0: and education use, including for instruction at the authors institution\nL3@P0: and sharing with colleagues.\nL4@P0: Other uses, including reproduction and distribution, or selling or\nL5@P0: licensing copies, or posting to personal, institutional or third party\nL6@P0: websites are prohibited.\nL7@P0: In most cases authors are permitted to post their version of the\nL8@P0: article (e.g. in Word or Tex form) to their personal website or\nL9@P0: institutional repository. Authors requiring further information\nL10@P0: regarding Elsevier’s archiving and manuscript policies are\nL11@P0: encouraged to visit:\nL12@P0-1: http://www.elsevier.com/copyrightAuthor's personal copy\nL13@P1: Linear Algebra and its Applications 437 (2012) 2429–2435\nL14@P1: Contents lists available at SciVerse ScienceDirect\nL15@P1: Linear Algebra and its Applications\nL16@P1: journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/laa\nL17@P1: Submatrix monotonicity of the Perron root<\nL18@P1: C.R. Johnsona, C. Marijuánb, M. Pisoneroc,∗\nL19@P1: a Dept. Mathematics, College of William and Mary, Williamsburg, VA 23187, USA\nL20@P1: b Dpto. Matemática Aplicada, E.T.S.I. Informática, Paseo de Belen 15, 47011-Valladolid, Spain\nL21@P1: c Dpto. Matemática Aplicada, E.T.S. de Arquitectura, Avenida de Salamanca 18, 47014-Valladolid, Spain\nL22@P1: ARTICLE INFO ABSTRACT\nL23@P1: Article history:\nL24@P1: Received 10 May 2011\nL25@P1: Accepted 22 June 2012\nL26@P1: Available online 25 July 2012\nL27@P1: Submitted by R.A. Brualdi\nL28@P1: AMS classification:\nL29@P1: 15A18\nL30@P1: 15A42\nL31@P1: 15B48\nL32@P1: 18B35\nL33@P1: Keywords:\nL34@P1: Comparing Perron roots\nL35@P1: Nonnegative matrices\nL36@P1: Variable submatrix\nL37@P1: It is known that increasing an entry of a nonnegative matrix non\u0002decreases (and generally increases) its Perron root. Motivated by a\nL38@P1: question raised by José Dias da Silva, we study the partial order on\nL39@P1: k-by-k nonnegative matrices in which A \u0002DS B if whenever A and B\nL40@P1: occur as submatrices in the same position in otherwise equal non\u0002negative matrices F and G, ρ(F) \u0003 ρ(G). We find that this partial\nL41@P1: order is equivalent to the entry-wise partial order. This is proven\nL42@P1: with some asymptotic results about the Perron root that may be of\nL43@P1: independent interest.\nL44@P1: © 2012 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\nL45@P1: 1. Introduction\nL46@P1: It is well known [1] that if an entry of an n-by-n positive matrix is increased, the Perron root\nL47@P1: increases. This may be improved by replacing “positive” with irreducible after the increase, and, in\nL48@P1: general, for nonnegative matrices if an entry is non-decreased, the Perron root does not decrease.\nL49@P1: Suppose now that instead of replacing an entry by a larger one, we consider replacing a proper square\nL50@P1: submatrix by another one with larger Perron root. Recently [2], Dias da Silva raised the question of the\nL51@P1: circumstances under which such a replacement of a principal submatrix would increase the Perron\nL52@P1: root of the full matrix. Of course, if the matrix is positive and the replacing submatrix “dominates” the\nL53@P1: replaced one, in the sense that some entries are larger and none smaller, then the full matrix will have\nL54@P1: < Partially supported by MTM2007-64704 and MTM2010-19281. ∗ Corresponding author.\nL55@P1: E-mail addresses: crjohnso@math.wm.edu (C.R. Johnson), marijuan@mat.uva.es (C. Marijuán), mpisoner@maf.uva.es\nL56@P1: (M. Pisonero).\nL57@P1: 0024-3795/$ - see front matter © 2012 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\nL58@P1-2: http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.laa.2012.07.006Author's personal copy\nL59@P2: 2430 C.R. Johnson et al. / Linear Algebra and its Applications 437 (2012) 2429–2435\nL60@P2: a larger Perron root. Otherwise, it may or may not. Examples are easily produced in which a larger\nL61@P2: Perron root for the submatrix results in a smaller Perron root for the full matrix (as was also noted in\nL62@P2: [3]) in all cases except when the submatrix is 1-by-1 or n-by-n.\nL63@P2: Here, our purpose is to give an answer to Dias da Silva’s question by studying the following preorder\nL64@P2: on k-by-k nonnegative matrices. We say that A \u0002DS B (“A is Dias da Silva less than or equal to B”) if,\nL65@P2: when two n-by-n nonnegative matrices F and G are equal, except in a k-by-k principal submatrix, with\nL66@P2: 0 < k < n, in which F is A and G is B, we necessarily have ρ(F) \u0003 ρ(G). Here, as usual, ρ denotes the\nL67@P2: spectral radius or Perron root. We denote the entry-wise (weak) partial order on nonnegative matrices\nL68@P2: (domination) by A \u0003 B if aij \u0003 bij for all i, j in A = (aij) and B = (bij). Our main result, surprising to\nL69@P2: both Dias da Silva and us, is that the Dias da Silva partial order and the domination partial order are\nL70@P2: equivalent.\nL71@P2: Theorem 1. If A and B are k-by-k nonnegative matrices, then A \u0002DS B if and only if A \u0003 B.\nL72@P2: Of course, if A \u0003 B, then A \u0002DS B by the facts mentioned earlier (and continuity of the Perron root).\nL73@P2: In the remainder of this work, we present the necessary analytical results about asymptotic behaviour\nL74@P2: of the Perron root (which may be of independent interest) that are necessary to prove the reverse\nL75@P2: implication.\nL76@P2: 2. Domination of diagonal entries\nL77@P2: Throughout this section, let\nL78@P2: F(x, y) =\nL79@P2: ⎛\nL80@P2: ⎜\nL81@P2: ⎜\nL82@P2: ⎜\nL83@P2: ⎝\nL84@P2: A11 a12 0\nL85@P2: aT\nL86@P2: 21 a22 x\nL87@P2: 0 y 0\nL88@P2: ⎞\nL89@P2: ⎟\nL90@P2: ⎟\nL91@P2: ⎟\nL92@P2: ⎠ , G(x, y) =\nL93@P2: ⎛\nL94@P2: ⎜\nL95@P2: ⎜\nL96@P2: ⎜\nL97@P2: ⎝\nL98@P2: B11 b12 0\nL99@P2: bT\nL100@P2: 21 b22 x\nL101@P2: 0 y 0\nL102@P2: ⎞\nL103@P2: ⎟\nL104@P2: ⎟\nL105@P2: ⎟\nL106@P2: ⎠ ,\nL107@P2: with A11 and B11 (n − 2)-by-(n − 2), a12, a21, b12 and b21 (n − 2)-by-1, and a22, x and y scalars. Let\nL108@P2: A =\nL109@P2: ⎛\nL110@P2: ⎝\nL111@P2: A11 a12\nL112@P2: aT\nL113@P2: 21 a22\nL114@P2: ⎞\nL115@P2: ⎠ and B =\nL116@P2: ⎛\nL117@P2: ⎝\nL118@P2: B11 b12\nL119@P2: bT\nL120@P2: 21 b22\nL121@P2: ⎞\nL122@P2: ⎠ .\nL123@P2: Our goal is to first show that for a nonnegative matrix of the form F(x, y), the Perron root is arbitrarily\nL124@P2: approximated by that of\nL125@P2: ⎛\nL126@P2: ⎝\nL127@P2: a22 x\nL128@P2: y 0\nL129@P2: ⎞\nL130@P2: ⎠\nL131@P2: for sufficiently large x and y. Given the explicit value of the latter Perron root, this will mean that\nL132@P2: ρ(F(x, y)) < ρ(G(x, y)) whenever a22 < b22 and x and y are sufficiently large.\nL133@P2: Lemma 2. For a22, x, y \u0004 0\nL134@P2: ρ\nL135@P2: ⎛\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[1305.1139] Counting Spectral Radii of Matrices with Positive Entries (https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.1139)\nciteturn4view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.1139\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 155\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Combinatorics\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 6 May 2013]\nL9: # Title:Counting Spectral Radii of Matrices with Positive Entries\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†J. A. Dias da Silva】, 【6†Pedro J. Freitas】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Counting Spectral Radii of Matrices with Positive Entries, by J. A. Dias da Silva and Pedro J. Freitas\nL14: \nL15: 【7†View PDF】 【8†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:The sum-product conjecture of Erd\\H os and Szemerédi states that, given a finite set $A$ of positive numbers, one can find asymptotic lower bounds for $\\max\\{|A+A|,|A\\cdot A|\\}$ of the order of $|A|^{1+\\delta}$ for every $\\delta <1$. In this paper we consider the set of all spectral radii of $n\\times n$ matrices with entries in $A$, and find lower bounds for the cardinality of this set. In the case $n=2$, this cardinality is necessarily larger than $\\max\\{|A+A|,|A\\cdot 